# Use Enums Instead of String Unions

## Rule

Prefer **TypeScript Enums** over string literal unions (`'a' | 'b' | 'c'`) for:

- Status values (e.g. chat status, submission status, session status)
- Roles (e.g. user role)
- Categories or fixed sets of domain values (e.g. skill category, sender type)

## Rationale

- Single source of truth and better refactoring
- Autocomplete and typo prevention
- Clear contract for API and components
- Easier to extend and document

## Convention

- Define enums in the central types module (e.g. `src/types/index.ts`).
- Use **string enums** so values stay compatible with APIs: `enum X { A = 'a', B = 'b' }`.
- Use PascalCase for enum names and for member names; values stay lowercase/snake_case when matching backend.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - string union
status: 'pending' | 'read' | 'replied' | 'archived';
sender_type: 'visitor' | 'admin';
role: 'admin' | 'super_admin';

// ✅ GOOD - enum
export enum ContactSubmissionStatus {
  Pending = 'pending',
  Read = 'read',
  Replied = 'replied',
  Archived = 'archived',
}
status: ContactSubmissionStatus;

export enum ChatMessageSenderType {
  Visitor = 'visitor',
  Admin = 'admin',
}
sender_type: ChatMessageSenderType;
```

## Usage in code

- Compare and assign using the enum: `msg.sender_type === ChatMessageSenderType.Visitor`.
- When receiving from API, cast if needed: `sender_type: data.sender_type as ChatMessageSenderType`.
